# Whispers Before the Silence: Shadows Over Blackthorn Manor

## Project Context
This is Episode 1 of a browser-based, choice-driven murder mystery game series published under the Āthariq label. Built with Vite, HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript.

## Architecture Overview

### Core Files
- `index.html` - Main HTML entry point with game container structure
- `style.css` - Dark, minimal styling matching Āthariq brand (dark background, soft off-white text)
- `main.js` - Game initialization and main game loop, wires together all systems

### Game Module (`game/` directory)
- `state.js` - Central game state management. All game state lives in `gameState` object. Contains time system, suspicion, clues, accusation data, and true solution.
- `scenes.js` - All game content: rooms, objects, suspects, weapons, endings. Data-driven, easy to extend.
- `logic.js` - Core game mechanics: navigation, examination, interrogation, accusation handling. Pure functions that read/write to gameState.
- `ui.js` - UI rendering system. Handles scene display, choice buttons, journal updates, modals, endings. Reads from gameState.

## Key Design Principles
- State is centralized in `gameState` object
- Content is data-driven in `scenes.js`
- Logic is separated from UI
- No backend - everything runs client-side
- Mobile-first responsive design
- Minimal UI, text-first presentation
- Dark theme, atmospheric, no bright colors

## Game Mechanics
- Time system: 12 turns, each action consumes time
- Suspicion system: Actions can raise suspicion
- Clue journal: Auto-updates as clues are discovered
- Accusation phase: Player must choose murderer and weapon
- Multiple endings based on correctness

## True Solution (Hidden)
- Murderer: Eleanor Blackthorn (id: 'eleanor')
- Weapon: Poison (id: 'poison')

## Extending the Game
- Add rooms to `rooms` object in `scenes.js`
- Add suspects to `suspects` object with dialogue trees
- Add objects with clues to `objects` object
- Add endings to `endings` object
- Update `gameState.trueSolution` if changing the solution

## Deployment
- Railway-ready: uses `$PORT` environment variable
- Build: `npm run build`
- Preview: `vite preview --port $PORT --host`

## Important Notes
- Never hardcode story logic into UI components
- Keep game state in `gameState` object only
- All content should be in `scenes.js`
- UI should only read from and trigger updates to gameState
- Time consumption happens in logic.js functions
- Clues are automatically added to journal when objects are examined



